.github.io/HydroFlow-12Channel-Water-Segmentation/dashboard.html", "https://www.kaggle.com/work/collections/19309568"], "pipeline_tag": "image-segmentation", "repo_id": "egyx/hydroflow-water-segmentation", "sha": "47c399ee0cf612759d3ad664239705dddfb7b85b", "tags": ["onnx", "remote-sensing", "satellite", "multispectral", "sentinel-2", "water-segmentation", "pytorch", "unet", "earth-observation", "geospatial", "image-segmentation", "en", "dataset:custom", "license:mit", "model-index", "region:us"]}, "organization": "egyx", "priority": {}, "published_at": "2026-10-09T23:09:00Z", "related_items": [], "scores": {"relevance": 0.4998}, "source": "huggingface", "source_id": "egyx/hydroflow-water-segmentation@2026-10-15T00:36:34Z", "summary": "HydroFlow is a production-grade deep learning model for high-resolution surface water extraction from **12-band Sentinel-2 multispectral satellite observations**. Based on a transfer-learning U-Net architecture with an ImageNet-pretrained **ResNet-34** backbone adapted for 12 spectral input channels, HydroFlow achieves **81.66% Global Pixel IoU** (and **82.10% Peak Validation IoU**) on strictly unseen satellite scenes.", "title": "egyx/hydroflow-water-segmentation", "topics": ["efficient_vision_models", "quantization", "foundation_model_adaptation", "super_resolution"], "updated_at": "2026-10-15T00:36:34Z", "url": "https://huggingface.co/egyx/hydroflow-water-segmentation", "venue": null}
{"authors": [], "discovered_at": "2026-10-15T01:33:08.974276Z", "id": "research_blog:article:1884af9e4b83dcb98358d07f", "kind": "article", "matched_terms": [], "metadata": {"action": "published", "categories": [], "classification": {"lexical_score": 0.0, "llm_model": null, "method": "semantic_profile", "semantic_model": "topic-profile-tfidf-v1", "semantic_similarity": 0.303602, "semantic_topic_scores": {"vision_language_models": 0.303602}}, "feed_id": "apple-machine-learning", "feed_name": "Apple Machine Learning Research", "links": []}, "organization": "Apple Machine Learning Research", "priority": {"source": 0.7}, "published_at": "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z", "related_items": [], "scores": {"relevance": 0.759}, "source": "research_blog", "source_id": "apple-machine-learning:1884af9e4b83dcb98358d07f", "summary": "Discrete diffusion models have emerged as strong alternatives to autoregressive language models, with recent work initializing and finetuning a base unimodal model for bi-modal generation. Diverging from previous approaches, we introduce the first tri-modal Masked Diffusion Models (MDM) pretrained from scratch on text, image-text, and audio-text data. We systematically analyze multimodal scaling laws, modality mixing ratios, noise schedules, batch-size effects and provide optimized inference sampling defaults. Our batch-size analysis yields a novel stochastic differential equation (SDE) based\u2026", "title": "The Design Space of Tri-Modal Masked Diffusion Models", "topics": ["vision_language_models"], "updated_at": null, "url": "https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/tri-modal-masked-diffusion", "venue": null}
{"authors": [], "discovered_at": "2026-10-15T01:33:08.974276Z", "id": "research_blog:article:d9edeedcb1b5d236cbbdc736", "kind": "article", "matched_terms": ["detection"], "metadata": {"action": "published", "categories": [], "classification": {"lexical_score": 0.6, "llm_model": null, "method": "lexical+semantic_profile", "semantic_model": "topic-profile-tfidf-v1", "semantic_similarity": 0.221285, "semantic_topic_scores": {"object_detection": 0.221285}}, "feed_id": "apple-machine-learning", "feed_name": "Apple Machine Learning Research", "links": []}, "organization": "Apple Machine Learning Research", "priority": {"source": 0.7}, "published_at": "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z", "related_items": [], "scores": {"relevance": 0.6}, "source": "research_blog", "source_id": "apple-machine-learning:d9edeedcb1b5d236cbbdc736", "summary": "Code-switching (CS), the alternation between languages within an utterance, is common in multilingual speech but underrepresented in pretraining corpora. Increasing CS coverage requires finding relevant utterances in large speech collections so that they can be included in training data. One way to find such utterances is to transcribe the speech and detect language alternation in the text, but this depends on obtaining accurate multilingual transcriptions. We propose a lightweight method that detects CS directly from speech. It uses frame-level language information in pretrained speech\u2026", "title": "Code-Switching Detection via Language-Selective Dimensions in Speech Foundation Models", "topics": ["object_detection"], "updated_at": null, "url": "https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/code-switching-detection", "venue": null}
